## 2. Specific Recommendations

### 2.1. Harden HTTP Headers (A04, A05)
Use **Helmet** to automatically set a robust set of security‑related headers.

```bash
npm i helmet
```

```js
const helmet = require('helmet');
app.use(helmet());                     // adds HSTS, CSP, X‑Content‑Type‑Options, etc.
```

If you need a custom Content‑Security‑Policy, you can add:

```js
app.use(
  helmet.contentSecurityPolicy({
    directives: {
      defaultSrc: ["'self'"],
      scriptSrc:  ["'self'"],
      // add more directives as needed
    },
  })
);
```

### 2.2. Enforce HTTPS & Secure Cookies (A02)
If this service is behind a reverse proxy (e.g., Nginx/Load‑Balancer) that terminates TLS, enable `trust proxy` and redirect HTTP to HTTPS.

```js
app.enable('trust proxy'); // needed when behind a proxy

// Redirect all HTTP -> HTTPS
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  if (req.secure) {
    return next();
  }
  res.redirect(`https://${req.headers.host}${req.originalUrl}`);
});

// Example: setting a secure cookie (if you ever use sessions)
app.use(require('express-session')({
  secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,
  cookie: { secure: true, httpOnly: true, sameSite: 'strict' },
}));
```

### 2.3. Input Validation & Sanitization (A03)
Even though the current routes are read‑only, future endpoints (e.g., `POST /users`) should validate every incoming field. Use **express‑validator** or **Joi**.

```bash
npm i express-validator
```

```js
const { body, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.post(
  '/users',
  [
    body('name')
      .trim()
      .isLength({ min: 1 })
      .withMessage('Name required')
      .escape(),
    body('email')
      .isEmail()
      .normalizeEmail()
      .withMessage('Valid email required')
  ],
  (req, res) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }
    // safe to use req.body now
  }
);
```

### 2.4. Authentication & Authorization (A01, A07)
Protect sensitive routes (`/users`) behind authentication. A simple JWT‑based approach works well for APIs.

```bash
npm i jsonwebtoken express-jwt
```

```js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const { expressjwt: expressJwt } = require('express-jwt');

// Middleware to protect routes
